  <!-- ============================================================ data -->
  <section id="data" class="mt-10 space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Data notes</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
      <li>
        <strong>Source.</strong> United Nations <em>World Population Prospects 2024</em>, medium variant, population on
        1 July by five-year age group and sex, 1950–2100. Years up to {meta.last_observed_year} are the UN's estimates;
        later years are projections. {meta.n_countries} countries and areas (every WPP “Country/Area” row) plus {nAgg}
        aggregates — the world, geographic regions and subregions, SDG regions, income and development groups.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Present borders.</strong> The UN back-casts every series within today's borders, so “Germany 1960”
        is the population of the territory of present-day Germany in 1960, not of either German state; the same holds
        for the successor states of the USSR, Yugoslavia, Sudan/South Sudan and others.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Names.</strong> Brief display names are used in the interface (Taiwan, Kosovo, South Korea, Türkiye…),
        with the UN name as the subtitle on each page (for example “China, Taiwan Province of China” and “Kosovo (under
        UNSC res. 1244)”). Any ISO code, UN name or common alias works in a URL.
      </li>
      {#if patch}
        <li>
          <strong>Togo interim update.</strong> On 19 January 2026 the UN issued an interim update for Togo after its
          2022 census; this site applies it (patch <code>{patch.id}</code>{patch.applied ? '' : ' — NOT applied in this build'}).
          Because the UN did not revise aggregates, the {patch.recomputed_aggregates.length} aggregates containing Togo
          are recomputed from their members here, so World, Africa, Western Africa and the income/development groups
          differ slightly from the UN's published totals. {patch.note}
        </li>
      {/if}
      <li>
        <strong>Microstates.</strong> {nMicro} of the {meta.n_countries} countries have fewer than 100,000 people in 2026.
        Their pyramids are noisy (a few hundred people move a band by a percent), so the default candidate floor is a
        population of 100k in the candidate's own year; set it to 10k or none to include them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nowcast years.</strong> Years after {meta.last_observed_year} up to the current year
        ({meta.last_observed_year + 1}–{app.currentYear}) are projections too, but near enough to count as “now”; they
        are included in the observed-era default and tagged “nowcast”. The current year is your device's clock.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Five-year bands, 16-bit shares.</strong> Ages are stored in 21 five-year bands with 100+ open-ended.
        Each pyramid's 42 shares are quantised to 16 bits with largest-remainder rounding so every row sums to exactly
        65,535 (a resolution of ≈ 0.0015 percentage points, far below the UN's own revision noise); totals are kept as
        floats so absolute numbers reproduce the UN's within a person or two. Files under <code>/data</code> are these
        derivatives, not the UN's files.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="projections" class="mt-10 space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why projections converge — and the era rule</h2>
    <p>
      The medium variant assumes fertility drifting towards replacement and mortality improving everywhere, so the
      pyramids of 2080 look far more alike than the pyramids of 1980 do. In a literal “any year 1950–2100” search the
      converged future crowds out history: Japan 2024's nearest any-year pyramid would be Latvia 2064. The default for
      cross-year searches is therefore <em>observed + nowcast</em> (≤ the current year) with projections opt-in; a chip
      beside the results counts the hidden projected years and one click includes them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Querying from a projected year</strong> flips the default: for <a class="underline" href={example('JPN', 2050)}>Japan 2050</a>
      the observed-only rule would exclude the query's own era and let the time-shift panel look only backwards, so
      the era defaults to “all” and the chip inverts to “projections included (query is a projection) · observed only”.
      The <code>era</code> URL parameter is written only when it differs from that default, so a shared link means the
      same thing on any device.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="development" class="mt-10 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Shape is not development</h2>
    <p>
      Two countries with the same pyramid share a demographic <em>moment</em> — a similar history of births, deaths and
      migration — not an economy, an institution or a future. Japan's twins in 2026 are Southern European; Qatar's
      are Gulf states with the same guest-worker bulge; a country matched with “Japan 2008” has Japan's age structure of
      that year and nothing else of Japan's. Time-shift matches are a way to see a shape in motion, and a starting
      point for questions, not a forecast: the site makes no claim about what a shape implies for growth, income or
      markets, and the language on every page is kept to what the data shows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reading" class="mt-10 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How to read a pyramid</h2>
    <p>
      Youngest at the bottom, oldest at the top; males to the left, females to the right; each bar is that group's
      share of the whole population, on an axis that stays fixed while you scrub through the years so bars grow and
      shrink in place. A wide base and narrow top (a triangle — <a class="underline" href={example('NER', app.currentYear)}>Niger</a>) means high
      fertility and a young population; straight sides (<a class="underline" href={example('USA', app.currentYear)}>United States</a>) a population
      near replacement; a top-heavy urn (<a class="underline" href={example('JPN', app.currentYear)}>Japan</a>) low fertility and long lives. Look for
      notches (a war, a famine, a one-child policy) and bulges (a baby boom, or migrant workers concentrated in the
      male 25–44 bands as in <a class="underline" href={example('QAT', app.currentYear)}>Qatar</a>), and watch them climb one band every five years.
      The right-hand column on each page names these features and the “stage” (expansive, stationary, constrictive) by
      median age; the ▸ marker flags a bar longer than the axis.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hosting" class="mt-10 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Hosting &amp; privacy</h2>
    <p>
      This is a static site on GitHub Pages: no server, no database, no account, no analytics, no cookies. Every
      search runs in your browser over small binary shards ({(meta.sizes.first_paint ?? 0) / 1000 | 0} KB for the first
      view; the {(meta.sizes.shares_d16z / 1e6).toFixed(1)} MB all-years corpus only when you ask for a cross-year search
      or open the time-shift panel). The whole view — country, year, constraints, comparison — is in the URL, so a
      copied link reproduces exactly what you see; the only thing stored on your device is the last country you looked
      at, for the “continue with” button on the home page. Exports (PNG, SVG, CSV) are generated client-side from the
      chart on screen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="attribution" class="mt-10 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Attribution &amp; licences</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      {#each meta.attribution as a, i (i)}<li>{a}</li>{/each}
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm">
      The full notice ships with the data: <a class="underline" href={noticeUrl}>NOTICE</a>. The UN does not endorse this
      site; files under <code>/data</code> are reshaped, share-normalised and 16-bit-quantised derivatives, not the
      original UN files. Design inspiration: <a class="underline" href="https://www.populationpyramid.net" rel="noopener">populationpyramid.net</a>
      (the fixed-axis, youngest-at-the-bottom pyramid and the year scrubber); no data or code from it is used. Code:
      MIT, <a class="underline" href="https://github.com/Eli-Jensen/population-pyramid-explorer" rel="noopener">github.com/Eli-Jensen/population-pyramid-explorer</a>.
      Image encoders used only offline for the experiment above: SigLIP 2 and DINOv2 (Apache-2.0).
    </p>
    <p class="text-xs text-muted">
      Corpus {meta.revision} built {built} ({meta.n_rows.toLocaleString('en-US')} pyramids); evaluation {evalBuilt} on the same data
      (hash <code>{meta.verdicts?.data_hash?.slice(0, 16) ?? '—'}…</code>{meta.verdicts?.stale ? ' — STALE relative to the shipped data' : ''}).
      Current year on this device: {eraChip(app.currentYear)}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-10 text-sm"><a class="underline" href={href({ kind: 'home' })}>← Home</a></p>
</article>
